docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:107:## T1: Claim-Scoped Temporal Graph Assembly
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:124:- [x] normalize relation inference for explicit and inferred `before`, `after`, `during`, `overlaps`, and `same_time` — `build_temporal_relation_registry` now marks explicit relations as `inference_mode="explicit"` and generates inferred `before`/`same_time` relations from date anchors with `inference_mode="derived_from_date_anchors"` for pairs not already covered by an explicit relation
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:125:- [x] formalize issue categories such as `missing_anchor`, `contradictory_dates`, `relative_only_ordering`, and `limitations_risk` — `build_temporal_issue_registry` now normalises `temporal_contradictory_dates` → `contradictory_dates`, `temporal_limitations_risk` → `limitations_risk`, etc. from the contradiction queue so rule profiles and downstream consumers see canonical category names
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:127:- [x] emit deterministic claim-level temporal graph summaries for packets and review payloads — `temporal_summary` with `relation_type_counts` and `relation_preview` from graph data in `_get_claim_reasoning_diagnostics`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:128:- [x] preserve relation previews and type counts from the graph, not from UI formatting logic — `relation_type_counts` carried through `consistency_summary` → `temporal_summary` → review payload
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:132:- claim-level temporal summaries are reproducible from one graph assembly path
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:133:- packet-level temporal readiness is traceable to underlying fact and relation IDs
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:134:- temporal warnings align with actual graph issues, not loose heuristics
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:143:- `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/test_t1_t3_temporal_next_steps.py -q`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:164:- [x] define a temporal rule profile contract with required events, optional events, deadlines, and defenses
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:167:- [x] expose rule-frame IDs in proof payloads so failures can be explained against concrete legal rules — `rule_frame_id` in proof bundles and `temporal_rule_frame_id` in element review items
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:168:- [x] document how claim-type timing rules differ from generic timeline consistency warnings — see `_TEMPORAL_ISSUE_FOLLOW_UP_PROFILES` in `complaint_analysis/temporal_rule_profiles.py`; claim-type rules (e.g. `retaliation_temporal_profile_v1`) evaluate ordered role-tagged facts against a legal frame (`retaliation_temporal_frame`) whereas generic timeline warnings come from issue-registry normalization of `missing_anchor`, `contradictory_dates`, and `relative_only_ordering` issue types without a claim-type gate
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:178:- if a claim type lacks a rule profile, the system falls back to generic temporal consistency rather than pretending legal sufficiency exists
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:203:- [x] emit theorem exports that reference fact IDs, relation IDs, and rule-frame IDs — `theorem_export_metadata` in `_build_temporal_proof_bundle` carries all of these
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:206:- [x] expose the same proof bundle previews through review payloads and operator UI — `temporal_proof_bundle_tdfol_preview` and `temporal_proof_bundle_dcec_preview` in flagged element items; `proof_bundles` dict for direct drilldown
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:230:Route temporal proof failures into specific testimony, document, or external-record follow-up actions.
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:241:- [x] map issue categories to recommended follow-up lanes — `_TEMPORAL_ISSUE_FOLLOW_UP_PROFILES` in `complaint_analysis/temporal_rule_profiles.py` maps `missing_anchor`, `contradictory_dates`, `limitations_risk`, `temporal_reverse_before`, retaliation-specific, and document-date gap categories to canonical lanes with `follow_up_target` and `proof_criticality`
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:243:- [x] rank follow-ups by proof criticality and legal timing impact — `rank_follow_ups()` in `temporal_rule_profiles.py` sorts high-criticality items first; `evaluate_temporal_rule_profile()` returns pre-ranked follow-ups
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:249:- each blocking temporal issue yields at least one explicit next action
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:251:- follow-up planning distinguishes chronology uncertainty from non-temporal missingness
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:269:Make temporal proof state a first-class readiness input across the review dashboard and document workflows.
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:281:- [x] expose proof bundle IDs and legal temporal frame references in review payloads — `temporal_proof_bundle_id`, `temporal_rule_frame_id`, `proof_bundles` dict all present in review output
docs/TEMPORAL_TIMELINE_PROOF_EXECUTION_BACKLOG.md:283:- [x] gate drafting readiness on legal temporal sufficiency, not only aggregate proof-readiness score — `_build_chronology_blocker_summary` now reads `temporal_rule_profile_failed_element_count` from `claim_reasoning_review` and sets `chronology_blocked=True` independently of issue counts
